.SS 5. clone and decode
\f[CR]lazy::clone()\f[R] gives
\f[CR]std::expected<cbor::owning<cbor::item>, error>\f[R].
It reads the whole item at the lazy, checks every head and builds one
\f[CR]cbor::item\f[R] for each data item.
\f[CR]cbor::decode(encoded)\f[R] does the same for the one top\-level
item in a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R].
The tree copies each text and byte string into its own memory zone.
It does not view the input, and it stays valid after the input ends.
.PP
An item has two data members: \f[CR]type\f[R] (a \f[CR]cbor::type\f[R])
and \f[CR]additional_information\f[R] (RFC 8949 3).
\f[CR]get<K>()\f[R] gives the value as
\f[CR]std::expected<cbor::item_alternative_t<K>, error>\f[R], where
\f[CR]K\f[R] is a \f[CR]cbor::type\f[R].
A \f[CR]K\f[R] that is not the \f[CR]type\f[R] of the item gives
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R].
.PP
