.PP
A tag 28 stays a tag item over the value it marks.
A tag 29 stays a tag item over its content.
The decode resolves neither tag and checks no index.
A tag 24 is a tag item over its byte string, and
\f[CR]cbor::encoded_cbor_data_item(tree, item)\f[R] gives a
\f[CR]std::expected\f[R] of the root of the embedded item, decoded as a
top\-level item of its own.
It throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R] for an item that is not a tag 24
of that tree.
For a tag 24 whose content is no byte string it gives
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R].
A count in a head reserves nothing: an array that claims more elements
than bytes are left gives \f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] before it reads an
element.
.PP
\f[CR]type()\f[R] of a lazy gives
\f[CR]std::expected<cbor::type, error>\f[R]: the major type, or for a
tag 2, 3 or a typed array of RFC 8746 the kind of that tag.
The data member \f[CR]type\f[R] of an item holds the same value.
A lazy and an item keep tags 28 and 29 as tags.
.PP
A bignum (tag 2 or 3) whose magnitude fits in 64 bits is the same value
as a basic integer (RFC 8949 3.4.3).
\f[CR]clone\f[R] and \f[CR]decode\f[R] make it an
\f[CR]unsigned_integer\f[R] or \f[CR]negative_integer\f[R] item, and
\f[CR]type()\f[R] of a lazy gives that type.
A larger bignum stays \f[CR]unsigned_bignum\f[R] or
\f[CR]negative_bignum\f[R].
A key 2(h\(aq01\(aq) is the same key as 1.
.PP
Such a bignum is a number everywhere: \f[CR]type()\f[R],
\f[CR]get<int>\f[R], \f[CR]get<double>\f[R], a path and a key lookup
read it as the integer, and no read gives it as a tag.
A bignum larger than 64 bits stays a tag item.
Its content is a byte string item that holds a copy of the magnitude.
The library has no type for big numbers and depends on no library for
them.
.PP
The decode reads every head of the item, so every limit counts each
head.
\f[CR]lazy::clone\f[R] and \f[CR]cbor::decode\f[R] read no byte after
the item, so a CBOR sequence (RFC 8742) is read item by item.
.SS 6. encode
\f[CR]cbor::encoder\f[R] writes one data item per call, in the order of
the bytes: \f[CR]unsigned_integer\f[R], \f[CR]negative_integer\f[R],
\f[CR]byte_string\f[R], \f[CR]text_string\f[R], \f[CR]array\f[R],
\f[CR]map\f[R], \f[CR]tag\f[R], \f[CR]simple_value\f[R],
\f[CR]floating_point\f[R] and \f[CR]typed_array\f[R].
An integer and a float take the width of their type, and a NaN keeps its
bits.
A count, a length and a tag number take the shortest head.
The encoder counts nothing: the counts of \f[CR]array\f[R] and
\f[CR]map\f[R] are yours.
.PP
Each call gives \f[CR]std::expected<void, error>\f[R].
After an error, and after \f[CR]finish()\f[R], every call throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
\f[CR]finish()\f[R] gives the size in bytes, or the pieces for an
\f[CR]iov_writer\f[R].
.PP
The target of the encoder is one of:
.IP \(bu 2
a container of bytes, for example \f[CR]std::string\f[R] or
\f[CR]std::vector<std::byte>\f[R]: the encoder appends to it;
.IP \(bu 2
a fixed span of bytes: the encoder writes into it, allocates nothing,
and gives \f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] when an item does not fit;
.IP \(bu 2
a \f[CR]cbor::iov_writer\f[R] (section 12);
.IP \(bu 2
a writer of your own with \f[CR]allocate\f[R], \f[CR]append\f[R] and
\f[CR]done\f[R].
An error of the writer comes back as a \f[CR]cbor::error\f[R]
(basics.md).
.IP
.EX
std::string out;
cbor::encoder e(out);
e.array(2);
e.unsigned_integer(std::uint8_t{1});
e.text_string(\(dqa\(dq);
e.finish();   \f[I]// 5: out holds 82 18 01 61 61\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Tags 28 and 29 mark shared values.
\f[CR]tag(28)\f[R] and \f[CR]tag(29)\f[R] write them as any other tag,
and no function of the library writes them by itself.
\f[CR]cbor::structs\f[R] encode writes no tag 28 and no tag 29.
.PP
\f[CR]cbor::encode(item, target)\f[R] writes an item with the additional
information that each item keeps.
Each integer, length and tag number keeps the width of its head on the
wire, tags 28 and 29 included.
A float keeps the width of its type, and a NaN keeps its bits.
A bignum that fits in 64 bits is decoded as a basic integer, and is
written in its shortest head.
It gives the size in bytes.
.SS 7. cbor::sequence
\f[CR]cbor::sequence\f[R] reads a CBOR Sequence (RFC 8742): top\-level
items one after the other, with nothing between them.
Its iterator gives \f[CR]std::expected<std::string_view, error>\f[R],
the bytes of one item.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// 1, \(dqa\(dq, [1, 2]\f[R]
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]auto\f[R] const e : cbor::sequence{owner})   \f[I]// owner: std::shared_ptr<std::string const>\f[R]
    *e;   \f[I]// the bytes of 1, then of \(dqa\(dq, then of [1, 2]\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
A last item that is not complete gives \f[CR]too_little_data\f[R].
The iterator gives the bytes that are left through \f[CR]encoded()\f[R],
so a reader of a stream can read them again with more bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
An item that is not well\-formed gives its error, and the sequence ends
there.
.IP \(bu 2
The sequence takes a \f[CR]std::shared_ptr<std::string const>\f[R], or a
\f[CR]std::shared_ptr<void const>\f[R] and a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R]
of the bytes that it keeps alive.
It keeps the owner alive.
.PP
Each element is a top\-level item of its own.
A tag 29 in one element does not reach a tag 28 in another.
\f[CR]lazy::view(owner, *e)\f[R] reads an element in place;
\f[CR]owner\f[R] is the \f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R] of the sequence.
.SS 8. diagnostic_notation
\f[CR]diagnostic_notation(encoded)\f[R] gives the item in the diagnostic
notation of RFC 8949 section 8.
A head that is longer than the preferred one carries its encoding
indicator.
.PP
