.SS 10. Tags 28 and 29
Tag 28 marks a value as shared.
Tag 29 with the index n stands for the value of the n\-th tag 28
(value\-sharing).
.IP \(bu 2
A lazy, \f[CR]clone\f[R], \f[CR]decode\f[R], \f[CR]at_path\f[R],
\f[CR]query\f[R] and \f[CR]diagnostic_notation\f[R] read tag 28 as a tag
over its content and tag 29 as a tag over its content.
They resolve nothing, check no index and give no error of their own.
A step and \f[CR]get\f[R] pass both tags, as they pass every tag that
they do not interpret.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]cbor::structs\f[R] decode reads them as tags that it does not
interpret (structs.md).
.IP \(bu 2
A lazy holds only its owner, its bytes and its offset.
Several threads read one lazy at the same time.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// owner: a std::shared_ptr<std::string const> with the bytes in the comment above each read\f[R]
\f[I]// [28(1), 29(0)]\f[R]
cbor::lazy::view(owner)\->at(1)\->type();                     \f[I]// cbor::type::tag\f[R]
cbor::lazy::view(owner)\->at(1)\->get<std::int64_t>();        \f[I]// 0\f[R]
cbor::diagnostic_notation(*owner);                           \f[I]// \(dq[28(1), 29(0)]\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS 11. Tag 55799
Tag 55799 (RFC 8949 3.4.6) marks bytes as CBOR.
\f[CR]lazy::take_ownership\f[R] and \f[CR]lazy::view\f[R] read one
leading tag 55799 as its content, in every width of the argument.
So \f[CR]clone\f[R] of that lazy gives the content.
\f[CR]cbor::decode\f[R] also reads one leading tag 55799 as its content,
so the root of its tree is the content.
A second tag 55799, and a tag 55799 inside an item, stay tags.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// owner: a std::shared_ptr<std::string const> with the bytes in the comment above each read\f[R]
\f[I]// 55799([1, 2])\f[R]
cbor::lazy::view(owner)\->at(1)\->get<std::int64_t>();   \f[I]// 2\f[R]
cbor::lazy::view(owner)\->clone();                      \f[I]// the array item [1, 2]\f[R]
cbor::decode(*owner);                                  \f[I]// the array item [1, 2]\f[R]
\f[I]// [55799(1)]\f[R]
cbor::lazy::view(owner)\->clone();                      \f[I]// an array that holds the tag item 55799(1)\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]e.tag(55799)\f[R] writes \f[CR]d9 d9 f7\f[R].
.SS 12. encode_iov
\f[CR]cbor::encode_iov(item)\f[R] writes a decoded item into a list of
pieces, not into one buffer.
It gives
\f[CR]std::expected<cbor::ref<std::span<iovec const>>, error>\f[R]: the
pieces in order as \f[CR]struct iovec\f[R], and their storage, which
lives as long as the result.
The pieces are for sending only: \f[CR]writev\f[R], \f[CR]sendmsg\f[R],
\f[CR]IORING_OP_WRITEV\f[R], \f[CR]IORING_OP_SENDMSG\f[R].
Hold the result until the call returns or the last completion of the
request comes.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]auto\f[R] const tree = cbor::decode(bytes);
\f[B]auto\f[R] const pieces = cbor::encode_iov(**tree);
writev(fd, (*pieces)\->data(), \f[B]static_cast\f[R]<int>((*pieces)\->size()));
.EE
.PP
The encoder writes into blocks of 16 KiB and copies each byte once; a
\f[CR]std::string\f[R] that grows would copy its bytes at each doubling.
.PP
\f[CR]cbor::encoder\f[R] over a \f[CR]cbor::iov_writer\f[R] writes item
by item.
A text or a byte string of 32 bytes or more that you hand over with its
owner is not copied: \f[CR]std::string&&\f[R] and
\f[CR]std::vector<std::byte>&&\f[R] are moved into the result, a
\f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R] or a \f[CR]cbor::ref\f[R] is kept.
\f[CR]finish()\f[R] gives the pieces.
.IP
.EX
cbor::iov_writer w;
cbor::encoder e(w);
e.text_string(std::move(long_text));   \f[I]// moved, not copied\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const pieces = e.finish();
.EE
